this week we're talking about task  queues big data and stackdriver welcome  to this week in cloud where we serve you  the lowest latency news just recently  launched for everyone  cloud task is a fully managed service  backed by App Engine you can easily  create new queues and offload any  asynchronous tasks giving you a  distributed task queue that you can  integrate with any of your apps with  built-in scheduling and deduplication as  well as an easy to integrate API cloud  tasks can be a great way to simplify  your architecture and focus on what  matters the users using your application  check out the details and get started  with a QuickStart guide all you need is  a GCP project with App Engine you may  already keep up to date with news about  Apache Hadoop and spark but cloud data  proc has lots of new features to try out  data proc is Google's fully managed to  dupe and spark service and has added  tons of integrations and features to  help you build Big Data solutions this  includes support for new versions of  software like Ubuntu and flink easy to  add components such as Jupiter and  Zeppelin and lots of Google Cloud  integrations like bigquery the full  details are in the blog post as well as  some links for you to try out data proc  for yourself but no matter where your  app runs you likely need to know how  it's running well that's one thing  stackdriver can help with Charles Baer a  Google cloud solution architect recently  published a blog post on how to build  your application and monitor for long  term metric analysis making it easier to  spot trends that you may not see from  reviewing short time frames  we've got the high level considerations  and an in-depth solution guide with a  reference implementation so you can  start to see the big picture and if you  want to learn a bit more about  stackdriver itself I definitely  recommend checking out the stack driver  sandbox it's an open source set of tools  that make it easy to get started and  learn what stack driver can do you can  clone the repo and simply run a script  to set up a full demo environment  including a micro services based demo  application and traffic generator giving  you the benefit of working in a real  scenario without disrupting your  production environment this is a great  way to learn more and practice how you  might do some sre tasks in Google Cloud  so don't delay and try it out if you  want to read more about these  announcements or see a full list check  out the link have any thoughts or ideas  leave us a comment or tweet us we'll see  you in the cloud  you
 

